## M1 — weakened loader check (kernel-side mutation)

Change: kernel/src/elf.rs line 207, `if e_phnum == 0 {` replaced with
`if e_phnum == usize::MAX {` (the zero-phnum fixture then falls through to
NoLoadSegments). Image rebuilt, harness run, dedicated captures:
build/k4b-mutant1.log and build/k4b-mutant1-serial.log.

Harness verdict (build/k4b-mutant1.log):

```
--- elf oracle C: boot the selftest image and re-derive the probe lines
  FAIL  selftest boot did not exit cleanly (rc=35; 35 = a gate failed)
        ok    load: staged-region sha256 equals the host oracle's digest
      ELF_KERNEL_PROBE layout seg0_base 0xffffff0008000000 seg0_delta 0x100 seg1_base 0xffffff0008200000 seg1_delta 0x108 entry 0x400100
      ELF_KERNEL_PROBE verdict staged_sha256 7feb498a250f5c0c4c8e867d1f56e202e0190d40d230fad3512eb3943e406ff3
      elf gate: 26 passed, 1 failed
      NOVA_SELFTEST_FAILED: 1 of 27 elf-gate checks failed
```

Gate's own lines from the mutant boot's serial log (build/k4b-mutant1-serial.log):

```
elf gate: ELF64 loader (K4b)
  FAIL  negative: phnum-zero refused as no-phdrs - got no-load-segments, want no-phdrs
elf gate: 26 passed, 1 failed
NOVA_SELFTEST_FAILED: 1 of 27 elf-gate checks failed
```

KILLED by layer C: harness rc=1, boot exit 35, gate 26 passed / 1 failed, and
the named failure is exactly the weakened rule (`phnum-zero refused as
no-phdrs - got no-load-segments, want no-phdrs`). Layers A/B/B2 stayed green
by design: the host spec oracle's phnum rule comes from the gABI, not from
elf.rs, so a kernel-side weakening is invisible to it — which is precisely
why the guest gate exists.

## M2 — fixture drift (data-side mutation)

Change: byte at offset 256 of kernel/fixtures/elf64-mini flipped to 0xA9
(dd conv=notrunc). Harness run, capture: build/k4b-mutant2.log. First lines:

```
--- elf oracle A: committed fixtures are byte-identical to the generator
  FAIL  fixture drift: elf64-mini
--- elf oracle B: spec oracle accepts the positive, rejects every negative
  ok    oracle: positive fixture accepted (VERDICT valid)
  ok    oracle: all 10 negative fixtures rejected (VERDICT invalid)
--- elf oracle B2: live oracle digest equals the gate's committed constant
  FAIL  oracle digest 46ae6f22f0844cc9c49389a84eda16b93c0b6da0b591aa3960f2751aa0495ec4 != gate constant 7feb498a250f5c0c4c8e867d1f56e202e0190d40d230fad3512eb3943e406ff3
--- elf oracle C: boot the selftest image and re-derive the probe lines
```

KILLED by layers A + B2 + C simultaneously: rc=1 — fixture drift named
against the generator's output, live oracle digest 46ae6f22... != gate
constant 7feb498a... (the digest cross-check did its job before any guest
run could agree with drifted data), and the guest gate failed on the same
bytes. Fixture restored via git checkout -- (only the flipped byte differed).

## What the two mutations establish

- M1: layer C (guest gate + exact-variant negative table) catches kernel-side
  policy rot that compilation and the host oracle cannot see. The negative
  table's exact-variant contract is load-bearing: the mutant still refused
  the fixture (fail-closed by accident), but for the WRONG reason, and only
  the named-variant check caught that.
- M2: layer A (fixture determinism) plus B2 (digest cross-check) catch data
  drift before any oracle or guest consumes it.

## Honesty notes

- Both mutants were each run once per layer; the pristine confirmation after
